      "id": "3776ab4d-34fa-48b7-8d2d-d4889c7cb166",
      "type": "MULTIPLE_CHOICE",
      "data": {
        "body": "\\textbf{Part A}: Three students present scientific arguments about finding the speed $v_0$ at which the block is launched into the air at the top of the spring. Which is/are correct?\n\n\\textbf{Heba:} \n\\textit{Claim:} I chose the block as the system and everything else as the surroundings. \n\\textit{Evidence:} Based on the situation described in the problem, I was told only the block is interacting with the spring and Earth while on a ramp. \n\\textit{Reasoning:} Therefore, I would have a change in kinetic energy from the block, change in potential energy from Earth, work from the spring, and work from friction of the ramp.\n\n\\textbf{Ghada:} \n\\textit{Claim:} I chose the block, spring, and Earth in the system, and the ramp in the surroundings. \n\\textit{Evidence:} Based on the situation described in the problem, I was only about the block, spring, and Earth with the ramp not experiencing any change. \n\\textit{Reasoning:} Therefore, I would have a change in kinetic energy from the block, change in potential energy from the spring, a change in potential energy from Earth, and work from friction of the ramp.\n\n\\textbf{Faiza:} \n\\textit{Claim:} I chose the block and spring as the system and everything else as the surroundings. \n\\textit{Evidence:} Based on the situation described in the problem, I was told only the block and spring are interacting with the Earth and the ramp. \n\\textit{Reasoning:} Therefore, I would have a change in kinetic energy from the block, work from the spring, work from Earth, and work from friction of the ramp.\n",
        "solution": [
          4
        ],
        "answerChoices": [
          {
            "id": "e4987370-34e4-4897-8bc1-77245d3d8b9f",
            "index": 0,
            "body": "\\textbf{\\underline{Heba}} is correct, but \\textbf{\\underline{Faiza}} and \\textbf{\\underline{Ghada}} wrong.",
